from common import topic

topic('G1',[
('What choice does largest-first coin greedy make?','The largest denomination not exceeding the remainder',['The smallest denomination always','Every denomination simultaneously','The coin used least often'],'It makes a locally largest feasible choice and repeats on the remainder.'),
('With coins {1,5,10,25}, what is the first greedy choice for amount 37?','25',['10','5','1'],'25 is the largest available coin at most 37.'),
('After selecting 25 from amount 37, what remains?','12',['11','13','25'],'Subtract the chosen coin: 37−25=12.'),
('Does a locally largest coin guarantee a globally minimum count for arbitrary denominations?','No',['Yes for every positive set','Yes if denominations are sorted','Yes if the target is even'],'A feasible local choice can obstruct a better combination.'),
('For amount 6 with unlimited coins {1,3,4}, what does largest-first greedy return?','4+1+1, three coins',['3+3, two coins','4+3, two coins','Six coins of value 1 only'],'Take 4, remainder 2, then two ones. The two threes are a better feasible solution.'),
('For amount 37 and coins {1,5,10,25}, what greedy sequence is selected?','25,10,1,1',['25,5,5,1,1','10,10,10,5,1,1','25,10,2'],'37→12→2→1→0 uses four valid coins.'),
('How can a denomination-at-a-time implementation avoid looping once per emitted coin?','Use quotient and remainder for each denomination',['Treat the answer count as zero','Ignore large targets','Use only subtraction by one'],'take=remaining//coin and remaining%=coin compute the multiplicity in constant arithmetic work per denomination.'),
('For target 14 and denominations {1,7,10}, compare greedy count with optimum.','Greedy 5; optimum 2',['Greedy 2; optimum 5','Both 5','Both 2'],'Greedy uses 10+1+1+1+1; 7+7 uses two coins.'),
('For k unsorted denominations and constant-time word arithmetic, what cost covers sorting and quotient/remainder scanning?','O(k log k) sorting plus O(k) scan',['O(target) sorting always','O(log k) total','O(k²) scan necessarily'],'Sort once; then process each denomination once. Explicitly listing every coin adds output cost.'),
('With coins {4,6} and target 8, greedy takes 6 and gets stuck. What does this show?','Greedy failure does not prove the target unreachable',['No solution exists','Coin 4 is invalid','Every target is unreachable'],'The legal combination 4+4 reaches 8 even though the greedy remainder 2 is impossible.'),
('If an implementation explicitly emits M chosen coins, what lower bound applies to its output work?','Ω(M)',['Ω(log M) only','O(1) regardless of output','Ω(M²) necessarily'],'Writing M output items takes at least linear work in M; multiplicity encoding changes output representation.'),
('What suffices to refute universal optimality of largest-first coin greedy?','One denomination set and target with a better feasible solution',['A slow implementation alone','One instance where greedy is optimal','A descending sorted coin list'],'A universal claim is disproved by a single valid counterexample, such as {1,3,4}, target 6.'),
('Malfoid says “sorted coins imply optimal greedy.” What is the missing issue?','Sorting chooses the order but does not prove the greedy-choice property',['Coins must be sorted alphabetically','The largest coin must be deleted','All denominations must be equal'],'Correctness depends on denomination structure and an argument, not the mechanics of sorting.')])

topic('G2',[
('For maximum-cardinality nonoverlapping intervals, which greedy key is used?','Earliest finishing time',['Earliest starting time','Largest duration','Largest starting time'],'Finishing earliest preserves the most room for remaining intervals.'),
('Under half-open interval convention [s,f), can [1,2) and [2,4) both be selected?','Yes',['No; touching always overlaps','Only if both have equal lengths','Only if 2 is an integer'],'Compatibility is next start≥last finish, allowing touching endpoints.'),
('What objective does the standard earliest-finish proof optimise?','Number of compatible jobs',['Total profit for arbitrary weights','Minimum room count automatically','Sum of durations necessarily'],'The unweighted activity-selection objective is maximum cardinality.'),
('After selecting a job finishing at 4, which next interval is compatible?','[4,6)',['[3,5)','[2,4)','[0,7)'],'A later selected job must start at or after the last finish.'),
('For jobs A=[0,3), B=[1,2), C=[2,4), D=[3,5), E=[4,6), what finish-order greedy set results?','B,C,E',['A,D','B,D,E','A,C,E'],'Finish order B,A,C,D,E: accept B, reject A, accept C, reject D, accept E.'),
('Why can an optimum first job be replaced by an earliest-finishing job?','The replacement finishes no later and preserves all later feasible jobs',['The replacement always starts earlier','All jobs have equal duration','It increases every profit'],'This exchange preserves cardinality and compatibility, enabling induction on remaining jobs.'),
('For [0,10), [1,2), [2,3), [3,4), how many jobs does earliest-start choose versus earliest-finish?','1 versus 3',['3 versus 1','Both 1','Both 4'],'Earliest-start takes [0,10) and blocks the others; earliest-finish takes the three touching short intervals.'),
('With n unsorted jobs, what standard complexity applies?','O(n log n) sort plus O(n) scan',['O(n²) scan necessarily','O(log n) total','O(2ⁿ) after sorting'],'Sort by finish time, then test compatibility once per job.'),
('Why does earliest-finish fail for arbitrary job profits?','More jobs need not mean higher total profit',['Profits affect interval endpoints','No profitable intervals can touch','Sorting becomes impossible'],'A long job of profit 100 may beat two compatible short jobs of profit 1 each.'),
('After repeated exchanges place all r greedy jobs into an optimum, why can the optimum not contain a later extra job?','Such a job would be feasible after the greedy finish and would have been accepted',['Optima are defined to have exactly r jobs','The last greedy job always finishes at infinity','Extra jobs must have negative profit'],'If any compatible remaining interval existed, the exhaustive finish-order scan would select one; this completes the maximal-count argument.'),
('Does a tie between equal finish times invalidate earliest-finish optimality for cardinality?','No; either earliest-finishing feasible choice preserves the exchange argument',['Yes; all finishes must be distinct','Yes; choose longest duration or fail','Only jobs with zero length may tie'],'The proof uses finish no later, so equality is sufficient under the stated compatibility convention.'),
('What distinguishes interval partitioning from this scheduling problem?','Partitioning assigns all jobs to rooms; scheduling selects a compatible subset',['They always have identical outputs','Partitioning discards all overlapping jobs','Scheduling minimises room count for all jobs'],'A different objective needs a different algorithm/proof; the lesson focuses on selecting maximum cardinality.'),
('A student rejects a job starting exactly at the last finish under [s,f). What is wrong?','The test should allow start=last finish',['The finish key must be reversed','The objective becomes weighted automatically','All equal endpoints are invalid input'],'Using > instead of ≥ rejects legal touching intervals and can reduce the selected count.')])

topic('G3',[
('What score orders items in fractional-knapsack density greedy?','Profit divided by weight',['Weight divided by profit','Profit times weight','Input position'],'Profit per unit capacity is the exchange-relevant quantity.'),
('What feature distinguishes fractional knapsack from 0/1 knapsack?','An item may be split',['Weights must be negative','Every item must be selected','Profit must equal weight'],'Divisibility permits replacing equal weights of material in the exchange proof.'),
('For weight 10 and profit 60, what is the density?','6',['600','1/6','50'],'60/10=6 profit units per weight unit.'),
('Under positive weights and nonnegative profits, when does the standard fractional scan stop?','When capacity is full or all items are exhausted',['Only after every item is taken fully','After the first item always','When the smallest density is positive'],'Stop at the actual limiting condition; total available weight may be below capacity.'),
('Capacity 50; items (weight,profit)=(10,60),(20,100),(30,120). What is density order?','Item 1, item 2, item 3',['Item 3, item 2, item 1','Item 2, item 3, item 1','All have equal density'],'Densities are 6,5,4.'),
('For that instance, what fraction of item 3 is selected after fully taking items 1 and 2?','2/3',['1/3','1','0'],'The first two use 30 capacity, leaving 20 of item 3 weight 30.'),
('What is the fractional optimum profit for that instance?','240',['220','160','280'],'60+100+(20/30)·120=240.'),
('Why can swapping equal weight from lower to higher density not decrease profit?','Profit change is weight times the nonnegative density difference',['All item profits are identical','The swap increases total weight','Fractions always have zero value'],'Equal transferred weight preserves capacity; higher density yields at least as much profit per unit.'),
('Capacity 5, items (4,12) and (4,8). What fractional optimum profit results?','14',['12','20','10'],'Take the density-3 item fully and one weight unit of density-2 material: 12+2=14.'),
('Capacity 100 but total available item weight is 60. What does greedy do with nonnegative profits?','Takes all items and leaves 40 unused',['Invents fractions beyond one','Duplicates the best item','Fails because exact filling is mandatory'],'Each item is available only up to its full quantity, and capacity is an upper bound.'),
('If two items have equal density, what does an equal-weight exchange do to profit?','Leaves profit unchanged',['Strictly increases it','Strictly decreases it','Makes weights negative'],'The density difference is zero; tie order need not affect optimum value.'),
('Which assumption is essential to the equal-weight exchange used here?','Divisible items',['Distinct item names','Integral profits','Sorted original input'],'Whole-item constraints may prevent the equal-weight replacement entirely.'),
('Malfoid reuses the fractional proof for 0/1 items. Where can the proof break?','A required partial-item exchange may be illegal',['Density becomes undefined for all positive weights','Capacity stops being an upper bound','Sorting cannot be done'],'The proof depends on legal fractional substitutions; indivisible items require a separate argument or exact method.')])

topic('D1',[
('What does memoisation save?','Computed answers indexed by subproblem state',['Only the original input size','Every recursive call stack forever','Only failed branches'],'Repeated requests reuse an answer instead of recomputing that state.'),
('What distinguishes tabulation from memoised top-down recursion?','It explicitly evaluates states in a dependency-compatible order',['It never stores results','It always uses exponential time','It changes the mathematical objective'],'Both can implement the same recurrence; evaluation order and call-stack use differ.'),
('For F(0)=0,F(1)=1,F(n)=F(n−1)+F(n−2), which subproblem repeats when expanding F(6)?','F(4)',['Only F(6)','A state with negative index necessarily','No subproblem repeats'],'F(6) calls F(4) directly, and F(5) also calls F(4).'),
('What must be stored when zero is a legitimate result?','A separate indication of whether a state was computed',['Only a zero-filled array with zero meaning absent','No cache entry for zero answers','The answer plus one as the mathematical result'],'A valid zero must not be mistaken for an uncomputed entry.'),
('How many distinct Fibonacci states F(0)..F(6) are there?','7',['6','13','64'],'Both endpoints are included: indices zero through six.'),
('For memoised Fibonacci through n, what standard time bound follows with constant-time arithmetic?','O(n)',['O(2ⁿ)','O(log n)','O(1)'],'There are n+1 states and at most two constant-time transitions per state.'),
('What space costs must memoised recursive Fibonacci account for?','Cache plus recursion stack',['Cache only; recursion is free','Only the return value','One word regardless of n'],'The recursion can have depth O(n), in addition to O(n) cached values.'),
('When can iterative Fibonacci use O(1) extra words?','When only the final value is needed and two predecessors suffice',['When every prior value must remain available','Only when n=2','When arbitrary reconstruction needs the full table'],'The recurrence depends on two previous values; keeping the whole history is unnecessary for final-value computation.'),
('Why can counting distinct states alone underestimate DP runtime?','Each state may examine many transitions',['Every state always costs one CPU instruction','No DP can have polynomial states','Distinct states equal recursive calls including repeats'],'Total work is states times transition cost, plus setup and reconstruction where applicable.'),
('Does top-down memoisation necessarily evaluate every theoretically possible state?','No; it may visit only states reachable from the requested answer',['Yes, even unreachable states','No; it evaluates no states','Only if all answers are nonzero'],'On-demand recursion can avoid unreachable states; a full bottom-up table may still fill them.'),
('Why does a cyclic recurrence need more than ordinary acyclic memoised recursion?','A state may depend on itself before its answer is finished',['Caches cannot store integers','All cycles imply no solution exists','Tabulation always ignores dependencies safely'],'One must resolve the dependency cycle with an appropriate formulation/algorithm rather than recursively waiting on unfinished values.'),
('If each of n states scans n predecessors, what total transition bound follows?','O(n²)',['O(n)','O(log n)','O(2ⁿ) necessarily'],'n states times O(n) work per state yields quadratic work despite memoisation.'),
('A student says “recursive means exponential, iterative means polynomial.” What is the correction?','Repeated-state work and dependencies determine cost, not syntax alone',['Every recursion is exponential','Every loop is polynomial','Memoisation is just a spelling change'],'Memoised recursion can be polynomial; loops can enumerate exponentially many objects.')])

topic('D2',[
('In the stipulated recurrence V(0)=V(1)=1 and V(n)=V(n−1)+V(⌊n/2⌋), what is V(0)?','1',['0','2','Undefined'],'The base is explicitly given and differs from the common Fibonacci zero base.'),
('For n=5, which second predecessor is used?','V(2)',['V(2.5)','V(3)','V(5)'],'Integer floor division gives ⌊5/2⌋=2.'),
('What evaluation order is valid for V(0)..V(n)?','Increasing index',['Decreasing index from n without recursion','Arbitrary order without checking dependencies','Only even indices'],'For n≥2 both n−1 and floor(n/2) are strictly smaller than n.'),
('What is V(2) for the stipulated recurrence?','2',['1','3','4'],'V(2)=V(1)+V(1)=1+1=2; one stored state may be used twice.'),
('What is V(4)?','5',['4','6','8'],'V(3)=V(2)+V(1)=3; V(4)=V(3)+V(2)=3+2=5.'),
('What is V(6)?','10',['8','12','13'],'V(5)=V(4)+V(2)=7, then V(6)=V(5)+V(3)=7+3=10.'),
('Does using V(1) twice when computing V(2) require two distinct cache entries?','No; one stored value is read twice',['Yes; duplicate mathematical states are mandatory','No; one summand must be deleted','Only if V(1)=0'],'State identity is the index; transitions may reference the same state more than once.'),
('With constant-time arithmetic, what is the time for a full table through n?','Θ(n)',['Θ(n log n)','Θ(2ⁿ)','Θ(log n)'],'There are n−1 computed nonbase entries and two table reads plus an addition per entry.'),
('Can the Fibonacci two-recent-values trick directly compute this recurrence for arbitrary n?','No; floor(n/2) may refer far beyond the two recent indices',['Yes because every recurrence has two terms','Yes because floor(n/2)=n−2 always','Only odd n need storage'],'The half-index dependency may require an older value that a two-value window discarded.'),
('What is V(8)?','18',['15','17','20'],'The table is 1,1,2,3,5,7,10,13,18: V(7)=10+3, V(8)=13+5.'),
('Why must the floor be specified rather than silently using ordinary real division?','The state table is indexed by integers',['Floor changes nothing for odd n','Arrays accept arbitrary fractional indices','The recurrence becomes Fibonacci without it'],'Odd n would otherwise request a noninteger state outside this definition.'),
('What additional qualification is needed if V(n) grows beyond one machine word?','The O(n) statement counts arithmetic operations; bit costs can grow',['No qualification; integers are always constant-size','Time must become O(1)','The recurrence stops being defined'],'The unit-cost operation model differs from exact bit-operation complexity for growing integers.'),
('A student sets V(0)=0 because that is familiar from Fibonacci. What is wrong?','This changes the specified recurrence problem',['All recurrences require a zero base','Only notation changes','V(0) can never affect any value'],'Use the supplied bases, not a superficially similar named sequence.')])

topic('D3',[
('For minimum coins with exact amount a, what does C[a] mean?','Fewest coins summing exactly to a',['Largest coin at most a','Number of different combinations','Total profit for capacity a'],'State meaning determines the recurrence and unreachable-value handling.'),
('What is the base C[0] for minimum-coin exact change?','0',['1','Infinity','The smallest denomination'],'The empty multiset makes amount zero with zero coins.'),
('How is an unreachable positive amount represented conceptually?','Infinity',['Zero','A negative coin count','The amount itself always'],'Infinity prevents an impossible predecessor from becoming a falsely good minimum.'),
('For coins {1,3,4}, what is C[1]?','1',['0','2','Infinity'],'A single coin of value one is optimal.'),
('For unlimited coins {1,3,4}, what is C[6]?','2',['3','4','1'],'Use 3+3; a single coin of value six is absent.'),
('Which recurrence is correct for unlimited positive coin values D?','C[a]=1+min C[a−d] over d≤a',['C[a]=max d≤a','C[a]=min C[d] over all d','C[a]=C[a−1] always'],'Choose a last coin d; the remaining exact amount is a−d, with one extra coin.'),
('For coins {3,4}, what are C[1] and C[6]?','Infinity and 2',['1 and 2','Infinity and Infinity','0 and 3'],'Amount one is unreachable, but 3+3 makes six.'),
('What information enables reconstruction of one minimum-coin solution?','A selected predecessor coin for each reachable amount',['Only the largest denomination','Only the final count without recurrence access','The input order alone'],'Following chosen last coins reduces the remaining amount until zero.'),
('For coins {1,3,4}, list C[0] through C[6].','[0,1,2,1,1,2,2]',['[0,1,2,1,1,2,3]','[1,1,2,1,1,2,2]','[0,1,1,1,1,1,1]'],'Amounts 3 and 4 use one coin; 5 uses 4+1; 6 uses 3+3.'),
('For k denominations and numeric target A, what full-table cost applies under constant-time arithmetic?','O(kA) time and O(A) table space',['O(k log A) time always','O(A²) space necessarily','O(2ᴬ) time necessarily'],'Each of A states tests at most k coins; predecessor storage stays linear in A.'),
('Why must nonpositive denominations be excluded from this simple increasing-amount DP?','They may fail to decrease the predecessor amount',['Positive coins cannot make zero','Zero coins are always optimal','Negative indices are valid cache states automatically'],'The acyclic dependency proof uses 0≤a−d<a for positive d.'),
('Two choices tie for the minimum at an amount. What is sufficient for returning one optimum?','Choose either tied reachable predecessor consistently',['Add both coin counts together','Declare the amount unreachable','Always discard both choices'],'Either tied predecessor yields the same optimum count; a tie rule only selects which solution is reconstructed.'),
('A student minimises the denomination itself rather than 1+C[a−d]. What is the flaw?','The objective is coin count including the optimal remainder',['The smallest coin always gives minimum count','All denominations have the same count effect on the remainder','DP needs no objective definition'],'Choosing a small face value alone ignores how many coins are needed to complete the exact amount.')])

topic('D4',[
('How does a subsequence differ from a substring?','A subsequence may skip positions but preserves order',['A subsequence may reorder characters','A substring may reorder characters','They always mean the same thing'],'LCS allows omissions, not permutation.'),
('For L[i,j] as LCS length of prefixes, what values lie in row zero and column zero?','Zero',['One','Infinity','The prefix length'],'An empty prefix has no nonempty common subsequence.'),
('When X[i−1]=Y[j−1], which recurrence is used?','L[i,j]=1+L[i−1,j−1]',['L[i,j]=L[i−1,j]+L[i,j−1]','L[i,j]=0','L[i,j]=i+j'],'Append the common final character to an optimum of the shorter prefixes.'),
('When the final characters differ, which recurrence applies?','max(L[i−1,j],L[i,j−1])',['1+L[i−1,j−1]','min(L[i−1,j],L[i,j−1])','L[i−1,j]+L[i,j−1]'],'At least one unequal final character is omitted; choose the better shorter-prefix solution.'),
('For X=ACGGA and Y=ACTG, what is the LCS length?','3',['2','4','5'],'ACG occurs in both; Y contains T absent from X, so a length-four subsequence is impossible.'),
('Which is a common subsequence of ACGGA and ACTG?','ACG',['AGC','ACT','GGA'],'ACG appears in order in both; ACT needs T in X, while the others violate Y order or multiplicity.'),
('With upper-neighbour tie preference on mismatches, where does backtracking move from the final A versus G cell for those strings?','Up',['Left','Diagonal and emit A','Stop immediately'],'At (5,4), upper and left both have value three; the stipulated tie chooses up.'),
('Why reverse characters collected during ordinary bottom-right LCS backtracking?','They were emitted from the end toward the start',['The recurrence stores characters in alphabetical order','LCS permits arbitrary reordering','The input strings must always be reversed'],'Diagonal emissions follow the subsequence backwards.'),
('For prefixes X=ACG and Y=ACTG, what is L[3,4]?','3',['2','4','1'],'The final G matches G, so add one to L[2,3]=2.'),
('What are full-table LCS time, table space and ordinary reconstruction time for lengths n,m?','O(nm), O(nm), O(n+m)',['O(n+m), O(n+m), O(nm)','O(nm), O(1), O(1)','O(2^(n+m)), O(nm), O(nm)'],'Each interior cell is constant work; each backtracking step decreases at least one index.'),
('Can rolling rows give LCS length with O(min(n,m)) table storage directly?','Yes, but standard full-table backtracking needs extra reconstruction work or information',['No; every length algorithm needs nm storage','Yes and all backtracking information is automatically retained','Only if the strings have equal characters everywhere'],'Only previous/current rows are needed for lengths; discarded decisions complicate simple reconstruction.'),
('For X=AB and Y=BA, why can different tie rules return different valid LCS strings?','Both A and B have optimal length one',['Only AB is optimal','The optimum length depends on tie rules','Ties prove the table is incorrect'],'Tie rules select among equal optima, changing the witness but not the optimum length.'),
('A student treats ACG as invalid because X=ACGGA contains gaps before its chosen G. What is wrong?','LCS requires order, not contiguity',['LCS allows reordering instead','Every LCS must use all input characters','Only equal-length inputs have subsequences'],'Skipping characters is permitted; confusing subsequences with substrings changes the problem.')])

topic('D5',[
('In 0/1 knapsack, how many times may each input item be selected?','At most once',['An unlimited number','Exactly twice','Any fraction between zero and one'],'The state must preserve the single-use constraint.'),
('What does P[c,j] represent in the lesson table?','Best profit with capacity at most c using the first j items',['Minimum weight of exactly j copies','Number of feasible subsets only','Profit using item j exactly c times'],'Capacity is an upper bound and the item prefix controls availability.'),
('For positive weights and optional nonnegative-profit items, what is P[0,j]?','0',['Infinity','j','The sum of profits'],'Zero capacity admits only the empty subset under positive weights.'),
('If item j has weight greater than c, what must happen?','Skip it: P[c,j]=P[c,j−1]',['Read P[c−w_j,j−1] with a negative capacity','Take a fraction','Set all earlier values to zero'],'The take branch is infeasible and must be guarded.'),
('What is the legal take branch in the 0/1 recurrence?','p_j+P[c−w_j,j−1]',['p_j+P[c−w_j,j]','P[c,j]+p_j','p_j+P[c+w_j,j−1]'],'Using the previous item column prevents item j from being selected again.'),
('Items (w,p)=(2,3),(3,4),(4,5), capacity 5. What is the optimum profit?','7',['8','6','5'],'Items one and two weigh five and give seven; other pairs are overweight.'),
('For only item (2,3) and capacity 4, what is the 0/1 optimum?','3',['6','4','0'],'The one available item can be used once; profit six would reuse it.'),
('For those three items and capacity 5, which pair is reconstructed from the optimum?','Items 1 and 2',['Items 1 and 3','Items 2 and 3','Two copies of item 1'],'The first pair is the only two-item feasible pair with profit seven.'),
('Why does a one-array 0/1 update usually iterate capacity downwards?','To read the previous item state before it is overwritten',['To allow unlimited reuse','To sort item profits','Because capacity zero must be last for every DP'],'Ascending updates can use an answer already containing the same current item.'),
('What changes if the take recurrence reads P[c−w_j,j] rather than the previous column?','It permits repeated use of item j',['It enforces single use more strongly','It solves fractional knapsack','It changes no result on any input'],'The same item remains available in the subproblem; this is the unbounded variant.'),
('What complexity follows from n items and capacities 0..C?','O(nC) time and O(nC) full-table space',['O(n log C) time always','O(C²) time independent of n','O(2ⁿ) table space necessarily'],'There are (n+1)(C+1) states, each comparing at most two constant-work alternatives.'),
('If take and skip profits tie, may reconstruction skip the current item?','Yes; it still returns an optimum',['No; every item in a tie must be taken','No; ties imply infeasibility','Only if its weight is zero'],'Equal values permit either legal branch; the witness can differ without changing optimum profit.'),
('A student fills unused capacity with invented copies of the best item. What constraint is violated?','Each input item is available at most once',['All capacities must be exactly filled','All profits must be equal','The table must be square'],'At-most capacity does not require exact filling, and spare capacity does not create new item copies.')])

topic('D6',[
('How many binary digits encode positive capacity C without leading zeros?','⌊log₂C⌋+1',['C','2ᶜ','⌈C/2⌉'],'Binary place values grow exponentially with digit count.'),
('What integer does binary 1000 represent?','8',['4','10','1000'],'1·2³+0·2²+0·2+0=8.'),
('What does pseudo-polynomial time mean here?','Polynomial in numeric magnitudes, potentially exponential in their binary encoding lengths',['Polynomial in every binary input length','Always factorial time','An approximate algorithm by definition'],'The representation of the input matters when capacity or target indexes a table.'),
('Why is O(nC) knapsack DP not automatically polynomial in binary input length?','C can be exponential in the number of bits used to encode it',['n is always exponential','Multiplication is forbidden','All DP algorithms are exponential'],'A short capacity field can denote a huge numeric table range.'),
('If C=2ᵇ, how does the capacity dimension scale with b?','2ᵇ',['b','b²','log₂b'],'The table indexes the numeric capacity, not merely its exponent or bit count.'),
('Does exact knapsack DP contradict NP-completeness of the binary-encoded decision version?','No; its numeric-capacity bound is pseudo-polynomial',['Yes; it proves P=NP immediately','Yes; all exact algorithms are polynomial','Only if the capacity is even'],'Exactness and polynomial binary-input running time are distinct properties.'),
('Why is ordinary LCS O(nm) polynomial in the explicit string input lengths?','n and m count explicitly supplied characters',['All DP bounds are polynomial by definition','Strings have no encoding','LCS uses capacity as a binary integer'],'The state dimensions are bounded directly by the actual explicit input lengths.'),
('For k coin types and target A in binary, what does the O(kA) coin-DP bound depend on?','The numeric amount A',['Only log₂A states','Only the largest coin label length','The number of feasible solutions'],'There is a state for every amount up to A, even though A may have a short binary encoding.'),
('If C is supplied in unary, how does the O(nC) bound relate to input length?','It is polynomial in that encoding length',['It becomes superexponential necessarily','It is independent of representation','It proves polynomial time for every encoding'],'Unary capacity alone uses Θ(C) symbols, making the table dimension bounded by input size.'),
('For n=20 and C=1024, how many interior capacity/item combinations does an n-by-C count give?','20,480',['200','1,044','20,971,520'],'20·1024=20,480; this operation estimate differs from the 11-bit capacity field length.'),
('Doubling a binary capacity from 2ᵇ to 2^(b+1) does what to its bit length and the capacity table range?','Adds one bit; roughly doubles the table range',['Doubles both bit length and range','Adds one state only','Squares the bit length'],'The encoding grows additively while numeric capacity grows multiplicatively.'),
('What qualification belongs to a word-operation O(nC) analysis?','Arithmetic operands must fit the assumed constant-time model',['It proves all arbitrary-length arithmetic is free','It is a bound on the number of feasible subsets','It forces every run to fill maximum possible capacity'],'Operation counts and bit costs are different; representation and implementation must be stated.'),
('Malfoid says “DP means polynomial, so O(nC) settles P versus NP.” What is wrong?','Polynomial in C is not necessarily polynomial in its binary encoding length',['DP cannot be exact','NP means no programs exist','O(nC) is always smaller than input length'],'The encoded-size distinction is exactly why the pseudo-polynomial bound does not settle that question.')])

topic('D7',[
('What must a DP state retain?','All information needed to determine valid future choices and value',['Only a convenient variable name','The final answer alone','Every prior operation regardless of relevance'],'Histories may be merged only when the retained state preserves the relevant future problem.'),
('What does optimal substructure say?','An optimum can be composed using optimal solutions of suitable subproblems',['Every local greedy choice is safe','Every subproblem is identical','All recursive code is efficient'],'The subproblems and their constraints must be defined so replacement is legal.'),
('What do overlapping subproblems make useful?','Caching repeated state answers',['Discarding every base case','Repeating all work','Sorting every table cell'],'Reuse removes repeated evaluation of the same mathematical state.'),
('Which strategy usually commits to one locally preferred choice?','Greedy',['DP enumeration of all relevant alternatives','Exhaustive subset search','Memoised comparison of every branch'],'A greedy method needs a proof that its chosen commitment is safe.'),
('Why must a replacement in an optimal-substructure proof be legal?','An infeasible replacement cannot contradict optimality',['Any better numeric score is enough regardless of constraints','Legality matters only for sorting','Optima have no constraints'],'The contradiction requires an improved feasible whole solution.'),
('Why is capacity alone insufficient for the standard 0/1-knapsack state across arbitrary histories?','Different remaining item sets can give different future choices',['Capacity must always be fractional','Profit never matters','All item sets with equal capacity are identical'],'The item-prefix/availability information is needed to prevent reuse and distinguish feasible continuations.'),
('Why can LCS use two prefix lengths as its state?','They uniquely specify the two remaining prefix subproblems',['They encode every possible subsequence explicitly','Characters no longer matter','The two lengths must be equal'],'The fixed input strings plus prefix endpoints determine the available ordered characters.'),
('What is the usual runtime accounting rule for a DP?','Number of states times work per state, plus relevant extra costs',['Number of recursion levels only','Input length regardless of transitions','Number of optimal solutions only'],'Initialization, reconstruction and recursion storage also need attention where material.'),
('Why does a shortest-path replacement argument need stated path assumptions?','A substitute may violate constraints such as simplicity or resource limits',['Every graph has negative edges','Path problems have no optimal substructure','Every shortest walk is Hamiltonian'],'Substructure depends on the legal subproblem; arbitrary replacement may not preserve the original constraints.'),
('Two histories reach the same location but have different remaining budgets. Can location alone always be a correct state?','No; feasible continuations may differ',['Yes because the location is identical','Yes if both histories have equal length','Only the earlier history matters'],'A budget-constrained problem needs sufficient resource information to distinguish future legality.'),
('A recurrence examines every legal last choice and combines it with an optimal smaller state. What else is needed for a correctness argument?','Show every feasible optimum is represented and every constructed option is feasible',['Only benchmark the code','Assume the largest index is optimal','Delete ties'],'Exhaustiveness and feasibility connect recurrence choices to the actual optimisation problem.'),
('When can divide-and-conquer avoid needing a memoisation table?','When recursive subproblems do not repeat materially',['Only when it uses no recursion','Whenever it contains two calls','Only when every branch returns zero'],'Memoisation targets overlap; independent branches can be solved directly without repeated-state savings.'),
('A student merges two states merely because their current profits match. What must be checked first?','Whether their legal future choices and resulting subproblem values are equivalent',['Whether the state names rhyme','Whether the profits are positive','Whether both came from recursion'],'Equal current scores alone do not establish equivalent future subproblems.')])
